.TH vncpasswd 1 "" "KasmVNC" "Virtual Network Computing"
.SH NAME
vncpasswd \- setup VNC users and passwords
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvncpasswd\fR \fB-u\fR \fIusername\fR [\fB-rwon\fR] [\fIpasswd-file\fR]
.br
\fBvncpasswd\fR \fB-u\fR \fIusername\fR \fB-d\fR [\fIpasswd-file\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vncpasswd
allows you to add users and passwords used to access VNC desktops. Multiple
users can be added, each with its own permissions.
You can grant view permission (\fB-r\fR), view and control permission (use of
mouse and keyboard) (\fB-w\fR), and user management permission
(\fB-o\fR).  When updating an existing user, the specified permissions replace
all previously assigned permissions. Any permissions not specified are revoked.
See OPTIONS below for details.

Its default behavior is to prompt for a VNC password and then store an
obfuscated version of this password to \fIpasswd-file\fR (or to
$HOME/.kasmpasswd if no password file is specified.)  The \fBvncserver\fP script
will ask you to add a user the first time you start a VNC desktop. HTTP Basic
Authentication will be used to ask for username and password, when you connect.

The password must be at least six characters long (maximum of 128 characters).
Note that the stored password is \fBnot\fP encrypted securely - anyone who has
access to this file can trivially find out the plain-text password, so
\fBvncpasswd\fP always sets appropriate permissions (read and write only by the
owner.)  However, when accessing a VNC desktop, a challenge-response mechanism
is used over the wire making it hard for anyone to crack the password simply by
snooping on the network.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.B \-u \fIusername\fR

Specify the username. Multiple users can be configured.

.TP
.B \-r

View permission. Allow the user to view remote desktop.
This permission is included with \fB-w\fR.

.TP
.B \-w

Write permission. Allow the user to view and control remote desktop with mouse
and keyboard. The \fB-r\fR option is not required when \fB-w\fR is specified.

.TP
.B \-o

Owner permission. Allow the user to add/delete users and change their
permissions.

.TP
.B \-d

Delete the user specified with \fB-u\fR. You need the owner permission for that.

.TP
.B \-n

Don't update the user's password, while updating permissions.

.SH FILES
.TP
$HOME/.kasmpasswd
Default location of the VNC password file.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Create a new user foo that can view and control remote desktop:
$ vncpasswd -u foo -w

.TP
Change user foo from write access to view-only access. Don't touch the password:
$ vncpasswd -u foo -r -n

.TP
Create a new user foo that can view remote desktop, but can't use mouse and keyboard:
$ vncpasswd -u foo -r

.TP
Create a new user foo that can add new users AND view and control remote desktop:
$ vncpasswd -u foo -ow

.TP
Delete user foo:
$ vncpasswd -u foo -d

.TP
Allow existing user foo to view remote desktop, use mouse and keyboard. Write permission (\fB-w\fR) is granted, all other permissions are revoked. Don't touch password.
$ vncpasswd -u foo -w -n

.TP
Remove all permissions from existing user foo and prompt for a new password.  The user will no longer be able to view or control remote desktops.
$ vncpasswd -u foo

